{"id":"708e0a61-0ecc-48df-a8a9-5c59839af058","object":"chat.completion","created":1784801218,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    items = payload['items']\\n    required = set(payload['required_failure_items'])\\n    unresolved_without = set(payload['unresolved_without'])\\n    \\n    def oracle(config_set):\\n        if not unresolved_without.issubset(config_set):\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        if required.issubset(config_set):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n    \\n    full_set = set(items)\\n    tests = 0\\n    outcome = oracle(full_set)\\n    tests += 1\\n    if outcome != 'FAIL':\\n        # initial must be FAIL, but handle gracefully\\n        return {'result': sorted(full_set), 'tests': tests, 'outcome': outcome}\\n    \\n    current_set = full_set\\n    granularity = 2\\n    \\n    while True:\\n        if len(current_set) == 1:\\n            break\\n        n = min(granularity, len(current_set))\\n        # partition current_set into n chunks (order by sorted list for determinism)\\n        sorted_list = sorted(current_set)\\n        chunks = []\\n        base = len(sorted_list) // n\\n        rem = len(sorted_list) % n\\n        idx = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            chunk_size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\\n            chunks.append(sorted_list[idx:idx+chunk_size])\\n            idx += chunk_size\\n        reduced = False\\n        for chunk in chunks:\\n            subset = set(chunk)\\n            complement = current_set - subset\\n            # test subset first\\n            res_sub = oracle(subset)\\n            tests += 1\\n            if res_sub == 'FAIL':\\n                current_set = subset\\n                granularity = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n            # test complement\\n            res_comp = oracle(complement)\\n            tests += 1\\n            if res_comp == 'FAIL':\\n                current_set = complement\\n                granularity = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if not reduced:\\n            if granularity >= len(current_set):\\n                # no single element removal causes FAIL => minimal\\n                break\\n            granularity *= 2\\n            if granularity > len(current_set):\\n                granularity = len(current_set)\\n    \\n    final_outcome = oracle(current_set)\\n    tests += 1\\n    # minimal set must be FAIL\\n    return {'result': sorted(current_set), 'tests': tests, 'outcome': final_outcome}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a Python source code for `solve(case)` that implements the ddmin algorithm with unresolved semantics. The case has items, required_failure_items, and unresolved_without. We need to minimize the failure-inducing configuration. The algorithm: start with all items, test if initial configuration FAILs (if not, maybe error?). Then perform ddmin: keep a candidate set, granularity = size/2? Actually typical ddmin: start with full set, if fails, try splitting into smaller subsets. But here we have required_failure_items that must be present to cause failure? Actually required_failure_items are items that are necessary for failure? And unresolved_without are items that if removed, cause UNRESOLVED? Let's parse the fixture: In case 0, required_failure_items = [\"d1\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without = []. In case 2, required_failure_items = [\"d4\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. So the oracle: given a set of items (configuration), we can determine outcome: FAIL if all required_failure_items are present? But also UNRESOLVED if any of unresolved_without are absent? Actually typical semantics: PASS if no failure, FAIL if failure, UNRESOLVED if cannot determine. But here we have defined \"required_failure_items\" and \"unresolved_without\". The problem statement: \"Minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration using the registered PASS, FAIL, and UNRESOLVED semantics and exact subset/complement schedule.\" The oracle is not given directly; we must simulate it. The fixtures show input shapes. So we need to define a deterministic oracle based on the payload. For a given case, the oracle: given a subset of items (configuration), we need to determine if it FAILs, PASSes, or is UNRESOLVED. The payload includes required_failure_items and unresolved_without. The oracle likely: \n- FAIL if all required_failure_items are present in the configuration.\n- UNRESOLVED if any of unresolved_without is missing from the configuration? But careful: In case 2, unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. So if d0 is missing, it's UNRESOLVED? And then FAIL condition still requires required_failure_items present. So the oracle: first check if configuration contains all items in unresolved_without? Actually \"unresolved_without\" suggests that if those items are absent, the test is unresolved. So if any item from unresolved_without is not in config, outcome = UNRESOLVED. Otherwise, if all required_failure_items are present, outcome = FAIL, else PASS. Also note that required_failure_items may overlap with unresolved_without? Possibly.\n\nWe need to implement ddmin that respects these semantics. The algorithm: start with full set of items. Test full set; it must be FAIL (per requirement). Then perform ddmin: we have a current set C that FAILs. We try to remove subsets. The typical ddmin algorithm from Zeller: minimize a set that causes failure. For each granularity, split C into n subsets (chunks). For each subset, test complement (i.e., C without that subset). If complement FAILs, then reduce C to complement and continue with same granularity. If no complement FAILs, increase granularity (double). When granularity equals size of C, we test individual elements. If no single removal causes FAIL, then C is 1-minimal.\n\nBut here we have UNRESOLVED outcomes. The algorithm says: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So we cannot reduce if complement is PASS or UNRESOLVED. Also when testing subsets before complements? The schedule: \"Test subsets before complements.\" Actually typical ddmin tests subsets? In ddmin, we test complements of subsets. But the description says \"Test subsets before complements\" and \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" That suggests we might test the subset itself first? Actually in ddmin we test the complement. But there is a variant: test the subset itself to see if it fails? But the objective is to minimize the failure-inducing configuration, so we want to remove elements. The typical algorithm: try to remove each chunk (subset) by testing the complement. If complement fails, then we can remove that chunk. If complement passes or unresolved, we cannot remove it. So we test complements. But the statement \"test subsets before complements\" might mean we first test the subset itself? That could be to check if the subset alone causes failure? But that is not typical for minimization. Alternatively, it might mean we partition into subsets, and for each subset we first test that subset (if it fails, we can reduce to that subset? But that would be a different algorithm). However, the typical ddmin for minimizing failure-inducing input uses complements. Let's read the description again: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL. Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" This is ambiguous. \"Test subsets before complements\" could mean when we partition, we test each subset (the chunk) first, and if it fails, we can reduce to that subset? But that would be minimizing to a smaller set that still fails. That is also a valid approach: try to find a smaller set that fails, rather than trying to remove. The typical ddmin (Delta Debugging) actually tests both the complement and the subset? Actually Zeller's ddmin algorithm tests the complement, and if complement fails, it reduces. There is also the concept of testing the subset itself to see if it fails, but that is used for simplification of inputs? I recall the ddmin algorithm: For each chunk, test complement. If complement fails, reduce to complement and restart. If no complement fails, then test each chunk individually? No.\n\nLet's think: The goal is to find a minimal set of items that still cause failure. The standard delta debugging: start with full set, split into n parts, test each complement. If any complement fails, replace C with complement and restart with n = 2. If no complement fails, increase n. When n >= |C|, test each single element removal. This yields a 1-minimal failure.\n\nBut there's also a variant that tests subsets first: if a subset fails, then you can reduce to that subset. That is actually more efficient because you can quickly find a smaller failing set. The description says \"test subsets before complements\" – maybe they want to test the subset itself first; if it fails, reduce to subset; else test complement. But then \"Only FAIL permits reduction\" – if subset fails, we can reduce to subset. If subset passes or unresolved, we can't reduce. Then test complement? That seems like two tests per chunk. Could be.\n\nGiven the need to implement exactly one function, and we have to follow the registered properties: \"registered_call_order\" suggests a specific order. The problem mentions \"exact subset/complement schedule\". Possibly the algorithm is: partition into subsets, test each subset in order. If a subset yields FAIL, reduce to that subset and restart. If no subset yields FAIL, then for each subset test its complement (i.e., C without that subset). If any complement yields FAIL, reduce to that complement and restart. Otherwise, double granularity. This is a common variant. The phrase \"test subsets before complements\" supports that.\n\nAlso note: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" So after a reduction, we reset granularity to 2? Or decrease? Typically after reduction, we start with n=2 again. \"otherwise double it\" means if no reduction, double n (up to current size). So we need to manage granularity (number of partitions). At each step, if we partition into n chunks, we test each chunk's subset (the chunk itself) first; if any fails, we reduce to that chunk and set n=2 (or decrease?). Then if none fail, we test each complement; if any fails, reduce to that complement and set n=2. If none fail, we double n (up to size of current set). When n equals size of set, we test individual elements? Actually when n >= |C|, each chunk size is 1. Then testing subset (the single element) - if it fails, that means the single element alone causes failure, so we reduce to that element. If not, testing complement (all but that element) - if complement fails, that means we can remove that element. So that yields 1-minimal.\n\nWe need to ensure to count tests. Return: \"1-minimal failure, test count, and terminal outcome\". So solve(case) should return a dict with keys: 'result' (the minimal set), 'tests' (number of tests performed), 'outcome' (the terminal outcome, should be FAIL). Since only FAIL permits reduction, the final minimal set must be FAIL.\n\nImplementation details: We'll need a function that given a list of items (the full set) and case payload, returns the outcome (FAIL, PASS, UNRESOLVED) for a set. We'll define a helper function `oracle(config)` that takes a set of items (or list) and returns one of 'FAIL','PASS','UNRESOLVED'. Based on case: if any of unresolved_without missing => UNRESOLVED. Else if all required_failure_items present => FAIL else PASS. Note: The required_failure_items are presumably the items that must be present to trigger failure. So if all are present, it's FAIL. If not, it's PASS (unless unresolved). Also initial configuration must be FAIL.\n\nWe need to validate: case must have items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. Use them.\n\nNow implement solve(case). It receives case dict with 'payload' containing the fields. We'll extract items list, required_failure_items list, unresolved_without list.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. full_set = set(items) (or list, but order not important)\n2. Test full_set. If outcome != FAIL, maybe raise error or handle? The problem says \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" but we should still handle: if not FAIL, then no reduction possible, return full_set, test_count=1, outcome. But probably all test cases will have initial FAIL. We'll assume it's FAIL, but we can still compute.\n3. Initialize current_set = full_set (as a set). tests = 1.\n4. granularity = 2 (start with two partitions). While True:\n   - If len(current_set) == 1: then it's 1-minimal. break.\n   - Partition current_set into n chunks, where n = min(granularity, len(current_set)). But careful: granularity may exceed length; we cap at length.\n   - For each chunk (subset):\n        a. Test subset itself. If outcome == FAIL: current_set = subset, tests +=1, granularity = 2, break out of inner loop and restart outer loop.\n        b. If not, test complement (current_set - subset). If outcome == FAIL: current_set = complement, tests +=1, granularity = 2, break.\n   - If after all chunks no reduction, then double granularity: granularity *= 2. If granularity > len(current_set), set granularity = len(current_set). Continue.\n   - If granularity already equals len(current_set) and no reduction after testing all chunks, then current_set is 1-minimal (since each element tested as subset and complement, but we already tested all-chunks? Actually when granularity = len(current_set), each chunk is a single element. In loop we test each subset (the element) and complement. If no reduction, then removing any single element does not cause FAIL, and no single element alone causes FAIL, so minimal. Break.\n5. Return result: {'result': list(sorted(current_set) maybe? Or maintain order? The items are ordered? The problem says \"ordered configuration\" but we are minimizing set? Actually ddmin works on sets, but the configuration is a set of items. The result should be a list of items in the minimal set. We can return as list, preserving original order? The input items list is ordered. We should probably return the minimal set as a list in the same order as they appear in original items? Or alphabetically? The fixture doesn't specify. Usually ddmin returns a subset. To be safe, return a list sorted by original order? We don't have original order mapping. We can just return list(current_set). But to be deterministic, sort the items (strings). The test likely expects a list. We'll return sorted list.\n   - Also include 'tests' count, and 'outcome' which should be 'FAIL'.\n\nWe need to carefully count tests: each time we call oracle, increment tests.\n\nEdge cases: If initial set fails, but after reduction we might get UNRESOLVED? No, only FAIL permits reduction. So the complement that causes reduction must be FAIL. So final outcome is FAIL.\n\nImplementation: We'll write solve(case) that returns dict as above. Use standard library only.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code string. Must escape properly.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: Partitioning: we have a list representation of current_set. We'll convert to list sorted for deterministic partition order. Partition into n chunks: use slice list into n roughly equal parts. For each chunk, compute subset = set(chunk), complement = current_set - subset. Then test.\n\nAfter each reduction, we break out of chunk loop and restart with granularity=2.\n\nWe need to ensure we don't get into infinite loop. The algorithm terminates when granularity equals len(current_set) and no reduction. That is the stopping condition.\n\nWe'll implement oracle as a nested function inside solve to access case payload.\n\nLet's write the Python code.\n\nConsider case where items list contains duplicates? No, likely unique.\n\nWe'll also handle edge where current_set becomes empty? That shouldn't happen because initial FAIL, and we only reduce to subsets that are FAIL, so not empty. But just in case, if empty, outcome would be PASS? But not needed.\n\nNow produce the JSON. The source code should be a single string with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nMake sure it's valid JSON: double quotes, backslashes escaped properly.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nLet's craft the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":817,"completion_tokens":3677,"total_tokens":4494,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":512},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3075},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":305},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}